WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Two children can earn the same language
score and still

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.240
need different evaluations.

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.000
The score tells you how they performed on
that task.

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.740
It does not explain the entire
communication problem.

5
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.680
Begin with the child's history, hearing,
language experience, and the

6
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.480
family's concern.

7
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.960
Then choose procedures that can answer that
concern.

8
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.440
Screening asks whether a deeper assessment
is needed.

9
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.360
A comprehensive evaluation asks what the
pattern means and what

10
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.140
should happen next.

11
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:30.670
Language has five connected domains:
semantics, syntax, morphology, pragmatics,

12
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:31.550
and phonology.

13
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:35.730
Meaning, sentence structure, word
structure, social use, and the sound

14
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.710
system work together.

15
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.530
The assessment must also fit the child's
development.

16
00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:43.470
With a preverbal child, look for gestures,
intentional communication,

17
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.230
responses, and shared attention.

18
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.410
Play and imitation can show foundations
that a picture naming

19
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:49.370
task would miss.

20
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.530
As language develops, add word
combinations, grammatical forms, more

21
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.250
complex directions, and narratives.

22
00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.650
Observing a familiar caregiver can reveal
abilities that a child

23
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.770
does not show with an unfamiliar examiner.

24
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.520
The lecture describes fewer than fifty
expressive words or no

25
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.940
word combinations at age two as an
increased risk signal.

26
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.080
Risk is not a diagnosis, and it does not
tell

27
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.820
you the child's future by itself.

28
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.960
Effective nonverbal communication, strong
comprehension, and age appropriate

29
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.340
speech sound accuracy are favorable signs
in the course material.

30
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.960
Evaluate the whole pattern.

31
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.820
A standardized test contributes a
comparison with its normative sample.

32
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.760
Informal observations and targeted tasks
reveal how language works in

33
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.380
daily life.

34
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.220
Dynamic assessment adds teaching and
retesting to investigate learning.

35
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:38.750
A useful language sample varies people,
activities, and contexts.

36
00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.570
Record it so that you can examine the
actual productions.

37
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.310
For morphology, separate free morphemes
from bound morphemes.

38
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.890
In the simple example dogs, dog stands
alone and the

39
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:50.630
plural ending attaches to it.

40
00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.610
Mean length of utterance is the total
number of counted

41
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.350
morphemes divided by the number of included
utterances.

42
00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.770
If a sample has one hundred twenty
morphemes across forty

43
00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.910
utterances, its mean length is three.

44
00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.070
That is a worked arithmetic example, not a
diagnosis or

45
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:06.750
an age equivalent.

46
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.900
Now change the lens without changing the
sample.

47
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:14.840
Semantics asks about word meanings,
diversity, substitutions, and word

48
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:15.440
finding.

49
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.960
Syntax asks how phrases, clauses, and
sentences are built.

50
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.040
Pragmatics asks how the child manages
communication with another person

51
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:23.980
in that situation.

52
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.140
Narrative analysis examines how a story is
organized and expressed.

53
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.880
These are complementary analyses.

54
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.460
Compare the formal results with the
family's observations.

55
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.240
When findings disagree, investigate the
reason.

56
00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.260
The goal is a defensible account of the
child's communication,

57
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.300
not a label produced by one number.
